        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 (in force April 2025), Muslim families follow Islamic rules: mothers receive physical custody by default, with custody extending to age 18. The child may choose their custodian at age 15. For non-Muslim expats under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, joint custody is the legal default from the moment of divorce. In Abu Dhabi, non-Muslims may also be governed by Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021, which gives equal custody rights to both parents until the child turns 16.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is the UAE a signatory to the Hague Convention on child abduction?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No. The UAE is not a signatory to the 1980 Hague Convention on the Civil Aspects of International Child Abduction. There are also no bilateral agreements between the UAE and countries like the UK, USA, Australia, or Canada on parental child abduction. This means if a parent takes a child out of the UAE to one of these countries without consent, there is no automatic return mechanism. You must pursue legal action in the country where the child has been taken.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can a mother take her children out of the UAE after divorce?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No, not without prior written consent from the father or a court order. Under Article 21 of Cabinet Resolution No. 122 of 2023, neither custodial parent may remove the child from the UAE without the other parent\'s documented consent or court approval. Courts can grant travel permission for up to 60 days at a time. Breaching this rule carries fines of AED 5,000 to AED 50,000 and possible imprisonment.',
