    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can an expat father get custody of young children in the UAE?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'It depends which law applies. Under Islamic personal status law, physical custody (hadana) of young children defaults to the mother, but the father keeps legal guardianship (wilaya) over major decisions, and can apply for custody if the mother is shown to be unfit or if the child chooses him at age 15. Under the 2022 civil law for non-Muslims, custody is joint and equal from the start, so a father who is present and stable can realistically secure a defined overnight schedule close to equal time. In both regimes, the surest way for a father to protect his role is a court order that spells out access days, holidays, passport control, and decision-making, rather than a vague "joint custody" label.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'My ex refuses to let me see my child despite the visitation order. What can I do?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Denial of contact is enforced the same way as any custody breach, through the Execution Court rather than the court that made the order. Keep a dated log of every missed visit and every message showing the refusal, then file for enforcement. The Execution Court can issue a formal warning, then fines, then police-assisted handover for the scheduled contact. Persistent, deliberate obstruction of access is itself a recognised ground to ask the court to review and even transfer primary custody, because it shows one parent is not supporting the child\'s relationship with the other.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How do I lift a travel ban on my child in the UAE?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Apply to the same court that issued the ban and show that the reason for it no longer holds or that it is being used punitively rather than to protect the child. Courts are more willing to lift or narrow a ban when you offer safeguards: a signed undertaking to return by a fixed date, return flight tickets, a financial bond, agreement to travel only to a country where a mirror order exists, or having a neutral third party hold the passport between trips. The ban can only be removed by court order, so it stays active at every airport and land border until the judge signs the release.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I relocate abroad with my child if the other parent objects?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Not without a court relocation order. If the other parent objects, you must apply to the Personal Status Court (or the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court) and prove the move serves the child\'s best interests, not just your own preference. A strong application sets out the child\'s new school, housing, and quality of life, and a concrete contact plan for the parent left behind: video calls, school-holiday visits, and who pays for the flights. Leaving the UAE with the child before the order is granted is a criminal offence and can trigger an Interpol notice, even if your visa has lapsed.',
      },
    },
  ],
};

